WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
- Somewhere in the hills just over there
is the Chauvet cave.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:06.070
It was discovered in 1994,
and inside is artwork

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:08.270
that's around 30,000 years old,

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:10.970
some of the best-preserved cave paintings
anywhere in the world.

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:12.620
It's a connection to ancestors

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.070
who lived tens of millennia
before history began.

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:18.720
Only a lucky few people
are ever allowed in that cave,

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.580
and almost all of them are scientific
researchers and conservators.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:26.690
There's no way that some guy with a camera
is ever going to get in there.

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.290
 ...right?

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:32.590
- The Chauvet cave
is the oldest painted cave we know of.

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:37.750
The cave has been rediscovered
the 19th of December 1994.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.750
It has been a tremendous discovery.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:44.770
It has pretty much changed everything
we thought until then.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:50.250
Roughly 1,000 representations,
paintings, drawings, engravings.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.480
The engravings are white in color,

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:59.450
the paintings are red, and the drawings,
which are made with charcoal, are black.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:04.090
- This isn't the real cave.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:06.340
It's a replica called Chauvet 2.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.040
When I say replica,
I don't just mean the art,

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.860
but the shapes of the cave walls
that it's painted on

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:13.890
were laser scanned and recreated.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.950
Cavers and historians would be able
to pick up things

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:19.010
that give it away, like
the suspiciously well-maintained lighting,

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:20.610
but to a layperson,

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:25.080
this is roughly the experience you'd get
standing in the original Chauvet cave

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.220
if it had been turned
into a tourist attraction,

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.886
complete with a lot
of other exhibits outside.

00:01:29.886 --> 00:01:34.270
So my question is: if you can
replicate something this accurately,

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:37.610
how much does it matter
that it's not the original?

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:41.120
- The original cave
was scanned several times,

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.650
so we've gathered a point cloud
that was made of 16 billion points.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.930
The original Chauvet cave
takes roughly

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:51.090
500 meters to get from the entrance
to the end of the cave,

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.910
so it is really huge.

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:54.140
In the original cave,

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.040
you could walk
for five, six, seven minutes

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.410
and see nothing.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:03.360
It would have been a waste
to recreate the cave as it was

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.430
because the point was
to make it more accessible,

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.150
so it has been reduced.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.900
If there's something
that we did not change,

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.560
it's just the drawings themselves,

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.010
the representations.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.830
They're where they're supposed to be,
you see them in the order

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:21.064
that you're supposed to see them
in the original cave.

00:02:21.064 --> 00:02:24.060
If at some point
we've changed sizes of some rooms,

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.830
we did not touch the walls on which

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:28.660
the paintings and the drawings were made.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.560
They're exactly in the same place

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.550
in the very same shape
as the original ones.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:37.210
The walls are concrete

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:41.850
and for the finer details such as bones,
stalactite, stalagmite, and so on,

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:45.990
it is resin modelled to the shape
of the original cave.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:49.050
The art is recreated by hand.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:53.976
They have been made
as precisely as could humanly be possible.

00:02:53.976 --> 00:02:55.970
To the millimeter, actually.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.480
They did not only do it by hand,

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.170
but they did use the same,

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:03.310
the very same type of wood
to make the charcoal.

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:06.730
The paintings, the reddish paintings,
they're made of hematite,

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:11.190
the same type of rock that was
used by the prehistoric painters.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.410
- Tourists aren't allowed
in the original cave

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:14.620
for very good reasons.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:18.400
That lesson was learned at Lascaux,
another cave a few hours west of here,

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.540
that was also filled
with prehistoric art.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:24.530
It was discovered in 1940,
and it was opened as a tourist attraction,

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:25.880
which destroyed it.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.500
Not completely, but: the presence
of 1,000 people a day

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.280
and the changes in temperature,
and humidity, and carbon dioxide levels,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.770
and contaminants
that all of them brought along,

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:37.970
well, it meant that after
tens of thousands of years of stability,

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:40.670
bacteria, fungi, and algae
took over the walls.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:43.880
Despite desperate conservation efforts
through the 20th century,

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:47.690
the Lascaux cave will never ever be
what it once was.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:52.250
- The prehistoric entrance
of the Chauvet cave is sealed off.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:56.650
Tonnes and tonnes of rock that collapsed
right on the entrance and sealed it off.

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:00.160
All the people, all the animals
that got inside of the cave

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:01.720
during prehistoric times,

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.660
they cannot possibly
enter the cave anymore,

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:08.610
and it has been so
for at least 21,000 years.

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:12.540
The fact that the cave has been isolated
from the outside world,

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.770
it made the atmosphere very stable

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:18.250
which allowed the paintings
and the drawings

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:20.180
to be preserved the way they are.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:24.100
-The trouble is,
tourists like visiting the real places.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:26.730
There is something
about actually being there,

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:30.462
about standing exactly where
an ancestor painted on a wall,

00:04:30.462 --> 00:04:32.880
and this cave can't provide that.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.130
But it isn't a Disney World mock-recreation,
it's pretty accurate,

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:38.170
and just a few kilometers away
from the original.

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:41.380
The sights,
the physical sensations are close,

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:44.870
except you don't have to be harnessed in
and climb down a big ladder to get here.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.660
If the original cave
had been open to tourists,

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:50.790
it would have been changed,
maybe devastated.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.460
Would this feel the same
if someone built this replica

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:55.910
in Paris, or in London, or Sydney?

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:58.600
How much does the context
of the experience matter?

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.010
How important is authenticity?

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.620
I don't have the answers to any of that,

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:05.500
but I can say that

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:08.320
a few times while I've walked around here
even with the rational knowledge,

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:09.570
that it's not the same place,

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:12.520
even knowing that I will exit
through the gift shop,

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:14.388
For a few seconds,

00:05:14.388 --> 00:05:15.890
a bit of my brain was fooled.

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:17.950
Although, maybe that's because
I got to be in here

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:19.300
alone after hours.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.310
Either way, I do like one idea,

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:23.410
that in another 30,000 years' time,

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:26.500
some future archeologist
is going to be really confused

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:29.580
by two identical caves
next to each other.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:34.570
- The huge amount of painted caves
we've discovered yet,

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:36.680
all the art they've left us,

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:41.270
it shows us that
those societies were in good health

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:45.490
because if they did have time
to do all of this,

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:47.460
it's because they had free time.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:51.300
It shows they do have a life,
they do have culture,

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.410
they do have art as we do today.

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:57.330
They might have not done it
for the very same reason,

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:58.450
but they do have it.

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:04.050
It shows that they do not just
merely survive, they live.

